        {
          "source_quote": "Σὺ πρῶτον εἰπὲ τί σοι δοκεῖ τὸ ἀγαθόν; ἢ καὶ τὸ δίκαιον;",
          "english_quote": "You first tell us what seems to you good, and what just.",
          "issue": "The draft replaces the second question’s disjunctive/additive ἢ καί, ‘or also,’ with an unqualified ‘and.’ It also makes the request for a conception of goodness and justice less explicit than the substantival Greek.",
          "suggested_revision": "Propose: ‘You tell me first: what do you think goodness is? Or justice, too?’ Preserve the alternative-question structure. The replacement requires fresh review.",
          "severity": "minor"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "reference": "18.2",
      "verdict": "revise",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": false,
        "no_added_claims": false,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "lexical_fidelity": false,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": false
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "ὡς σοὶ δοκεῖ αὐτὸς ὢν ἐπυθόμην ποιήσομαι τὰς· ἀποκρίσεις.",
          "english_quote": "I will give the answers as you wish, although I was the one who asked.",
          "issue": "The contextual interpretation is defensible, but the smooth English conceals the irregular juxtaposition of participial ὢν and finite ἐπυθόμην. The draft supplies no disclosure of that construction. The selected print reads ὢν, not the relative pronoun ὃς that would more directly support ‘the one who asked.’",
          "suggested_revision": "Add a source-quoted note explaining that the English interprets the printed construction contextually, with Peter answering despite having himself asked. If ὃς for ὢν is mentioned, identify it strictly as a proposed correction, not the supplied reading. The printed punctuation in ‘τὰς· ἀποκρίσεις’ is not grounds for an OCR diagnosis or a verification block.",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "τῷ μὲν πᾶσιν παρέχειν, διαφόροις δὲ χαρίζεσθαι",
          "english_quote": "in providing for all but granting different gifts",
          "issue": "The draft selects a gifts/instrumental interpretation of διαφόροις without explaining it. The dative can instead identify recipients: ‘to different people,’ or potentially ‘to the distinguished/worthy.’ This materially affects whether the contrast concerns the gifts or their recipients; the Greek does not simply print an accusative object meaning ‘different gifts.’",
          "suggested_revision": "Explicitly compare the draft with ‘by providing for everyone but granting favors to different recipients.’ If retaining ‘different gifts,’ explain the instrumental construal and its contextual support, and preserve the recipient interpretation in a note. If choosing recipients, explain the alternatives ‘different’ and ‘distinguished/worthy.’ Any revised wording and notes require fresh review.",
          "severity": "major"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "καὶ μακρόθυμον ταύτη μᾶλλον, εἰ ἁμαρτωλοῖς μὲν μετανοοῦσιν χαρίζεται τὰ ἁμαρτήματα, εὖ πράξασιν δὲ καὶ ζωὴν αἰώνιον ὑπογράφει.",
          "english_quote": "and all the more patient in this way: he forgives sinners their sins when they reorient and promises eternal life also to those who have acted well.",
          "issue": "The conditional εἰ is not represented. The colon turns the conditional argument into an unqualified assertion about forgiveness and eternal life. The draft’s ‘when’ represents the participle μετανοοῦσιν; it does not account for εἰ.",
          "suggested_revision": "Propose: ‘and all the more patient in this respect, if he forgives the sins of sinners who reorient and also promises eternal life to those who have acted well.’ Review the revised sentence afresh, including the scope of the condition.",
          "severity": "major"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "reference": "18.3",
      "verdict": "accept",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": true,
