    },
    {
      "reference": "18.4",
      "verdict": "revise",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": true,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "lexical_fidelity": true,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": false
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "ὅστις κατὰ ἀριθμὸν τῶν υἱῶν Ἰσραήλ, οἳ εἰσῆλθον εἰς Αἴγυπτον (οἵ εἰσιν ἑβδομήκοντα), καὶ πρὸς τὰ ὅρια τῶν ἐθνῶν περιγράψας γλώσσαις ἑβδομήκοντα",
          "english_quote": "In accordance with the number of the sons of Israel who entered Egypt, who are seventy, and marking out the nations’ boundaries by seventy languages",
          "issue": "The contextual delineation is defensible, but the English silently makes ‘the nations’ boundaries’ the object of ‘marking out.’ In the supplied construction, τὰ ὅρια belongs to the prepositional phrase πρὸς τὰ ὅρια, while περιγράψας has an unexpressed object. The attachment of the number and boundary phrases is not explained by the existing note about retaining ‘Israel.’",
          "suggested_revision": "Add a source-quoted note explaining the elliptical object and the attachment adopted. Compare the draft with a more source-transparent formulation such as ‘having marked out divisions by seventy languages, in accordance with the number … and in relation to the nations’ boundaries.’ If retaining the draft, explain why the boundaries are understood as the contextual object. This is an interpretive construction issue, not evidence of unreadable print or an additional lacuna.",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "ἀπὸ τοῦ υἱοῦ τοῦ πάντων κυρίου",
          "english_quote": "from the Son, the Lord of all",
          "issue": "The appositional interpretation is contextually defensible, especially after the Son has already been called ‘Lord,’ but it is not the only material construction. The consecutive genitives also permit ‘from the Son of the Lord of all.’ The draft does not disclose this alternative or explain its choice.",
          "suggested_revision": "Retain the appositional interpretation only with a note preserving ‘from the Son of the Lord of all’ as the alternative and explaining that the earlier designation of the Son as Lord favors the chosen attachment. Review the amended passage and note afresh; the existence of this alternative does not itself require blocking.",
          "severity": "major"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "reference": "18.5",
      "verdict": "revise",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": true,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "lexical_fidelity": true,
